#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100582 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a185b05c-242a-58bb-9c5d-e94e51c15e77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph With One Area"}] :question/id #uuid "1894cde0-6e6d-51ad-a739-34254f787898" :question/math-academy-id "e-10250" :question/problem "![](images/b2/b2f034132460f2a6b3a26eb52bd4129fb13ea5a871d766815b83047217b97b3b.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,5]$. The graph of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8$. Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,5]$ given that $f(-2)=4$." :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-2,5)$, and\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\neq 0\n$$\n\non this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,5$.\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-2) &= 4 \\\\\nf(5) &= f(-2) + \\int_{-2}^{5}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&= 4 - 8 \\\\\n&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2$, we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = \\min\\{4, -4\\} = -4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = -4\n$$\n\nand the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx_{\\text{min}} = 5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100702 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100862 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b321acb7-1241-5c20-b307-21d18764ef2c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph With More Than One Area"}] :question/id #uuid "73bb5c40-4aa3-57a7-bb63-8be5275bc1f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-444" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[1,9]$. The graph of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown below, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $3$, $5$, and $4$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,9]$ given that $f(1)=5$.\n\n![](images/a8/a86baf9a7cddb9ea44de15d5c87ba01a1b8d68eb233950fb5f22f73ae4dd661b.png)" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,9]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (1,9)$, and $f'(x) = 0$ at $x=3$ and $x=6$. Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,3,6,9$.\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= 5 \\\\\nf(3) &= f(1) + \\int_{1}^{3}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&= 5 + 3 \\\\\n&= 8 \\\\\nf(6) &= f(1) + \\int_{1}^{6}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&= 5 + 3 - 5 \\\\\n&= 3 \\\\\nf(9) &= f(1) + \\int_{1}^{9}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&= 5 + 3 - 5 + 4 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2$, we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = \\min\\{5, 8, 3, 7\\} = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = 3\n$$\n\nand the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx_{\\text{min}} = 6\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100703 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9187b2a-8c34-5dda-8ae4-2c861f1c0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value by Computing Areas Under Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "41d966eb-f45c-5a8c-9d75-5ad6dfcd202d" :question/math-academy-id "e-445" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0cac30d21bdd2f2cc3aeaa0ed907d6eeb9ff78b3dae92cad40b56fab76bd7237.png)\n\nThe function $f$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,2]$. The graph of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown above. Find the minimum value of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,2]$ given that $f(-4)=0$." :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-4,2)$, and $f'(x)=0$ at $x=0$. Therefore, the candidates are\n\n$$\nx = -4, 0, 2\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nThe function is made up of two trapezoids and one triangle. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/a6/a676f3c026bdae86c6c353c3c5a38f6922dfe5b7dc83d955c1ee2d392d9f2021.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-4) &= 0 \\\\\nf(0) &= f(-4) + \\int_{-4}^{0}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&= 0 - 7 \\\\\n&=-7 \\\\\nf(2) &= f(-4) + \\int_{-4}^{2}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&= 0 - 7 + 1 + 1.5 \\\\\n&=-4.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2$, we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = \\min\\{0, -7, -4.5 \\} = -7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = -7\n$$\n\nand the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{min} = 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100862 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b321acb7-1241-5c20-b307-21d18764ef2c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph With More Than One Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94fbf80a-62f3-5263-a7b7-239f19b0bdec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100012} {:answer